## Free Backend Setup (Firebase)

The site works standalone with demo data and a "call us to order" fallback
on the contact form. Connecting Firebase turns on:

- **admin.html** — a login-protected dashboard where the owner adds/edits @@CATALOG_ITEM_PLURAL_LOWER@@,
  uploads photos, and views order inquiries — no GitHub or code editing needed.
- **Live @@CATALOG_ITEM_PLURAL_LOWER@@** — the public site pulls data from Firebase instead of
  the hardcoded demo list, so admin edits show up immediately.
- **Real order inquiries** — the contact form saves submissions to Firebase instead
  of just showing a placeholder message.

It's genuinely free at this scale (Firebase's Spark plan) and there's no server to
host or pay for — the site talks to Firebase directly from the browser.

**Setup steps (one-time, ~15 minutes):**

1. Go to https://console.firebase.google.com and create a new project (free Spark plan).
2. **Firestore Database** → Create database → start in production mode.
3. **Storage** → Get started (accept the default bucket).
4. **Authentication** → Sign-in method → enable **Email/Password**. Then go to the
   **Users** tab → Add user → enter the owner's email and a temporary password
   (Firebase requires **at least 6 characters**). Copy the new user's **UID** —
   you'll need it in the next step.
5. **CRITICAL — grant admin access.** Being able to log in is *not* the same as
   being allowed to edit the site: the security rules (`firebase/firestore.rules`,
   `firebase/storage.rules`) only allow writes from UIDs listed in an `admins`
   collection, and that collection can only be edited from the console (never
   from the website itself — that's what keeps a random signup from editing
   your data). In **Firestore Database → Data**, create a collection named
   `admins`, then add a document whose **Document ID is the user's UID** from
   step 4 (the document's contents don't matter — an empty document is fine).
   Skip this and the owner will be able to log in but every save/add/delete will
   silently fail with a permissions error, and the dashboard will show "No Access".
6. **Project settings** (gear icon) → General → "Your apps" → click the `</>` (Web)
   icon → register an app (nickname doesn't matter, check "Also set up Firebase
   Hosting" if offered) → copy the `firebaseConfig` object it gives you into
   `js/firebase-config.js`, replacing the placeholder values.
7. **Firestore Database** → **Rules** tab → paste in the contents of
   `firebase/firestore.rules` → Publish.
8. **Storage** → **Rules** tab → paste in the contents of `firebase/storage.rules` → Publish.
9. Deploy the site to **Firebase Hosting** (see "Deploying" below) — same project,
   same login, so hosting + database + photo storage + admin login are all in one place.
10. Visit `yoursite.com/admin.html` and log in with the email/temporary password from
    step 4. On first login, a banner prompts the owner to set their own password —
    they can do that any time from the **Account** tab (needs their current password
    and a new one at least 6 characters). If they ever forget it, "Forgot your
    password?" on the login screen emails them a reset link directly from Firebase.

Once @@CATALOG_ITEM_PLURAL_LOWER@@ are added in the dashboard, they replace the demo listings on the
public site automatically. `admin.html` is excluded from search engines
(`robots.txt` + a `noindex` tag) since it's not meant to be publicly discoverable —
it's still only reachable by whoever has the login, but there's no reason for it
to show up in search results either.

To add a second admin later (e.g. an employee), repeat steps 4-5 for their account.

## Replacing photos without Firebase (no code editing required)

The images shipped in `images/` are generated placeholders (simple colored graphics
using the brand colors), not real photos. Swap them any time by uploading a
replacement with the **same filename**:

| File | Where it's used |
|---|---|
| `images/store-display.svg` (or `.jpg`) | Home page hero background, About page photo |
| `images/storefront.svg` (or `.jpg`) | Home page "Locally Owned" section, social-share preview image |
| `images/logo.svg` | Logo shown in the nav and browser tab |

To use a real photo instead of the placeholder, upload a `.jpg`/`.png` with the
matching base name and update the `src=`/`content=` references in the HTML files
(a find/replace of the filename extension across the repo is enough).

